WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.330 --> 00:00:02.205
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Are
you spending more time

00:00:02.205 --> 00:00:04.740
monitoring your services
than developing new ones?

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:07.110
Find out how you can
use Anthos to improve

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:09.120
the observability
of your services

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.220
and become more productive.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:13.140
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:19.980
I'm Priyanka, and
welcome to Anthos 101.

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:23.160
Software is revolutionizing
every industry.

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:26.370
It doesn't matter if your
company sells tires, delivers

00:00:26.370 --> 00:00:30.150
pizza, is a bank, or helps
recruit new employees.

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:33.490
Software is now a key
part of what you deliver.

00:00:33.490 --> 00:00:36.060
So let's look at how
you can use Anthos

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:40.230
to be productive while
keeping your customers happy.

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:43.830
Anthos gives you access to a
service operations dashboard

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:46.140
that greatly simplifies
what it takes

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:48.330
for you to keep
your applications up

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:51.000
and running at peak
efficiency to serve

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.220
those wonderful
customers of yours.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:58.180
Key to making this happen are
what we call golden signals.

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:00.460
If you can only
measure four metrics

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:04.400
of your user-facing systems,
focus on, first, latency,

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:06.970
which is the time it takes
to service a request.

00:01:06.970 --> 00:01:09.070
It's important to distinguish
between the latency

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:12.700
of successful requests and the
latency of failed requests.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:16.420
Second is traffic, which is
the measure of how much demand

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:18.730
is being placed on
your system measured

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:21.760
in high-level,
system-specific metrics.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:24.730
Third is errors, which
are the rate of requests

00:01:24.730 --> 00:01:27.200
that fail, either explicitly--

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:30.520
for example, HTTP
500s; implicitly--

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:33.490
example, HTTP 200
success responses

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:37.240
but coupled with the wrong
content; or by policy--

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:40.900
example, if you committed to
one-second response times,

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:44.170
any request over one
second is an error.

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:48.640
And fourth, saturation, which
is how full your service is,

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:50.650
a measure of your
system fraction

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:54.760
emphasizing the resources
that are most constrained.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:57.250
These golden signals
are the cornerstone

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:59.950
of establishing and
embracing SRE best

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:02.660
practices at your organization.

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:05.470
If you are looking into
adopting these methodologies,

00:02:05.470 --> 00:02:08.419
the service mesh should
be your solution.

00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:10.479
So what is a service mesh?

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:13.240
A service mesh provides
a transparent and

00:02:13.240 --> 00:02:15.730
language-independent
way to flexibly

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:19.490
and easily automate
application network functions.

00:02:19.490 --> 00:02:23.020
And it is a foundational
component of Anthos.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:26.050
Anthos Service Mesh helps
you monitor and manage

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:30.340
service interactions across all
your different environments.

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:33.520
Adopting micro services
has lots of benefits,

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:36.400
like increasing
velocity and scaling.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:39.010
But it also adds complexity.

00:02:39.010 --> 00:02:42.250
If different teams own
each of these components,

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:46.000
how do you track the total
latency for that transaction

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:50.320
or ensure that traffic between
these services is secure?

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:53.080
As an operator, you
have a tough job

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:55.510
to do in order to
give your customers

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:58.180
the amazing experience
they deserve.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:00.310
Anthos Service
Mesh brings to you

00:03:00.310 --> 00:03:04.810
a topology view of all the
services deployed in the mesh.

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:07.630
You can see what's
running, who calls whom,

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:10.270
and metrics for
each interaction.

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:12.850
Anthos Service Mesh
generates these metrics

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:15.730
without any changes
to your application

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:19.840
by tracking all communications
between your services.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:22.480
On a single screen, it
becomes easy for you

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:26.240
as an administrator to spot
problems and see metrics,

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:30.250
including all four of the
golden signals we talked about.

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:33.490
But also, you can
go as deep as needed

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:36.670
with detailed information
at the per-node level,

00:03:36.670 --> 00:03:39.830
including CPU and memory usage.

00:03:39.830 --> 00:03:42.310
These metrics help
your app developers

00:03:42.310 --> 00:03:44.650
as they look to do
performance tuning

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:47.380
for the services they own.

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:50.490
This is very useful to
you as operators too.

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:53.170
Anthos Service Mesh lets
you set Service Level

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:55.630
Objectives against
any of these generated

00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:57.540
metrics we talked about.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:03:59.980
And once you set
up this SLO, you

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:03.580
can immediately see how
the service is performing.

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:07.300
And once you have SLOs, you can
start managing your deployment

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:09.640
risk using error budgets.

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:13.210
This enables you to catch
problems early and fix them

00:04:13.210 --> 00:04:16.250
well before your
customers run into them.

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:19.010
What makes this so
powerful is that now you

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:22.370
can break down the problem of
keeping your deployment running

00:04:22.370 --> 00:04:25.220
into smaller, more
manageable pieces,

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:27.950
reducing operational complexity.

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:31.790
Anthos Service Mesh also
gathers all logs and traces

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:35.510
for your services, bringing
them together into one place.

00:04:35.510 --> 00:04:38.750
Generally, you might have
one system for metrics,

00:04:38.750 --> 00:04:41.790
one system for logs, and
something else for tracing.

00:04:41.790 --> 00:04:46.160
So correlating between them
is difficult or impossible.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:49.760
Anthos helps you bring
them all together.

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:50.710
There you have it.

00:04:50.710 --> 00:04:53.800
With Anthos, you can
adopt SRE best practices

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:58.110
and improve your observability
using golden signals.

00:04:58.110 --> 00:05:01.450
Try Anthos Service Mesh and
the Observability Dashboard

